abstract:
If you’re building for developers, you already know that coding agents are one of the main ways devs access your API, SDK, and docs. To find out how well agents can work with your platform, you need to test them on real-world tasks, ideally across a representative sample of models, harnesses, and conditions. Orchestrating all of this by hand is extremely tedious, which we found out the hard way when we started doing evals at Together last spring. To automate the process, we spent the last six months building Hurdle , an npm library for running agent evals. Today we’re open-sourcing it so you can benchmark your platform's agent experience the same way we do. Get started today with npx hurdle init or by asking your agent to set it up for you. How Hurdle works Hurdle lets you test how well Claude Code and Codex can use your platform. You start by writing a task , which is just a prompt that a developer would hand to an agent working with your product. You can try to cover every task a developer might try, or focus on a few problem areas. Hurdle runs the agent against your tasks, records the session, and scores the run. Exactly how scoring works is up to you. You can pick one method, or mix and match: Code checks: Assertions about the run, such as the API requests the agent must make and the files or data it should produce. Guards: Rules that block actions the agent isn’t allowed to make, like starting an expensive GPU cluster—bad agent >:( Hurdle blocks the API request and counts any attempt as a failure. Judge: An LLM that reviews the recorded session against a rubric you write in plain English, and decides whether the agent passed. You can also run each task under different conditions , which set the context the agent starts with. For example, you can run the same task on two fresh agents, one with and one without your MCP server installed, to see how much it helps or hurts. Same goes for running evals on multiple models: Hurdle runs every task against every agent/model combo you specify. Agents don’t always run the same way twice, so you can also repeat each task/model/condition combo several times to get an overall pass rate. Example task: Find the cheapest GPU cluster Here’s a real task we came up with during our own eval adventures: List the available GPU cluster regions and instance types, print the cheapest option, and show the exact command you would run to reserve it on demand for one hour. Do not actually reserve it. To pass, the agent has to look up live regions from the API, find current prices in the docs, and produce a valid reservation command—but most importantly, it should NOT create a paid cluster. Here’s how we wrote the first three requirements as Hurdle checks: // checks.mjs
import { defineChecks } from "hurdle"; // Hurdle calls this function after the agent finishes
export default defineChecks((run) => { // Passes only if the agent made a successful GET /v1/compute/regions call // Request checks only work for sandboxed runs run.expect.request({ id: "regions", method: "GET", path: "/v1/compute/regions", status: 200, }); // The rubric for the LLM judge run.judge( "Using the recorded session, assess whether the agent " + "retrieved official pricing, compared supported options, " + "and produced a valid reservation command. " + "Mark fail if the required pricing evidence is missing. " + "Note confusing API responses and recovery attempts." );
}); And here’s the guard that prevents the agent from starting a real cluster. Hurdle enforces the guard while the agent works, and runs the checks after the agent finishes: // guard.mjs
import { defineGuard } from "hurdle"; export default defineGuard((guard) => { // Block any request to create a cluster // The check fails if the agent tries the request guard.block.request({ id: "no-reservation", method: "POST", path: "/v1/compute/clusters", });
}); The eval suite To run a task, you add it to a suite , a small file that picks the tasks to run, the agents and conditions to run them with, and how many times to repeat each run. For the GPU cluster task, we’re using both a deterministic code check and an LLM judge, so we set the suite's verdict to hybrid and name a judge model: // example.suite.mjs
import { defineSuite } from "hurdle"; export default defineSuite({ // The API under test, and the name of the env var that holds its key. api: { authorizationCredential: "TOGETHER_API_KEY", hosts: ["api.together.ai"], }, tasks: ["./tasks/gpu-cluster"], // The two models we tested in June. agents: [ { runner: "claude-code", model: "claude-opus-4-8" }, { runner: "claude-code", model: "claude-sonnet-4-6" }, ], // Use conditions to run each task with and without our GPU clusters skill. conditions: { baseline: {}, "with-skill": { skills: [{ name: "together-gpu-clusters", sourceDirectory: "skills/together-gpu-clusters" }], }, }, // Run every combination of task, agent, and condition 15 times. repeats: 15, // A run passes only if the code checks and the judge both pass. verdict: "hybrid", judge: { model: "claude-opus-4-8" },
}); Run and view results npx hurdle run runs the suite. Adding --view opens the results viewer when the suite finishes: npx hurdle run --view Hurdle runs each task for every agent and condition in the suite, as many times as repeats says. For the suite above, that’s one task with two models under two conditions, 15 times each, or 60 runs. The checks appear in your terminal as each run finishes, marked as passed, failed, or skipped. The viewer shows the suite’s headline numbers at the top: pass rate, average duration, and average cost per run. Below that, a grid breaks down the pass rate by task and condition, so you can see which combos are failing: Click into a run to see exactly what the agent did, including a full transcript, a list of the agent's tool calls and API requests, plus the result of every check and the judge's final assessment. Hurdle saves the results to .hurdle/ in your project directory, so you can reopen the viewer by running npx hurdle view . Local runs For quick tests on tasks you trust, you can run your evals locally, much as you would if you were prompting the agent yourself. Hurdle starts the claude or codex CLI on your machine in a temporary workspace, hands it the task's prompt and files, and records the session. Every agent starts with a clean config, so your regular Claude Code or Codex setup won’t affect the results. Local runs come with two caveats: First, Hurdle can’t see or block the agent’s API requests, so you can’t use guards or request checks. But more importantly, the agent runs as you , so it can read local files and use your real API keys. For anything complex or risky, you should always use a sandbox. Sandboxed runs For production evaluations, Hurdle uses the Together Sandbox API to spin up isolated environments where coding agents can execute code, install dependencies, and interact with APIs without accessing your local machine. Each run gets a fresh sandbox, so agents start from a clean environment rather than inheriting files or state from previous runs. Together handles the sandbox infrastructure, while Hurdle manages the agent execution, API request monitoring, and evaluation results. When you start a sandboxed run, Hurdle launches a local proxy called Guardian, then opens a single WebSocket to Together, which boots a fresh Together Sandbox for the run. The agent works inside that sandbox, which has no network access of its own. Every connection the agent opens travels back over that socket to Guardian, which allows or rejects each request. Your keys never enter the sandbox—the agent gets random stand-ins, and when it calls your API, Guardian swaps in the real key and forwards the request. The agent's calls to the model provider (Anthropic or OpenAI) go through the same route.

"I fear not the man the man who one shots 2500 symphonies with Claude, rather I fear the man who 2500 shots a single symphony with Claude" So, I got bored and wanted to push Opus to its limit with respect to music composition. Basically, I did the meme: You can watch a full virtual instrument rendering of the composition on youtube here, which is likely higher quality than the version you'd get streamed from x.com: Watch the symphony 25:14 · four movements · plays here from YouTube Movement I 0:00 Movement II 7:19 Movement III 13:14 Movement IV 18:01 YouTube picks the picture quality from your connection and the size of the player. For a sharper score, choose 1080p or higher under the gear icon, or go full screen. 4 movements 929 bars 22,979 notes 25 min of music 35B Opus tokens, roughly https://youtu.be/AQT9LJR_FEc The full scores, as PDF: Movement I PDF Movement II PDF Movement III PDF Movement IV PDF The source of the scores, the critics' reviews and the scripts are on GitHub: github.com/halfprice06/claude-symphony These are virtual instruments, so the audio quality is not as good as if an orchestra recorded this live. But its pretty good, and considering Claude did all of the mockup and rendering in a DAW with pro-level VSTs, I'd say the result is still pretty impressive. The whole thing used around 35 billion Opus tokens. We used Claude Code Projects (beta) for this, and I find it really useful for project-based coding. The orchestrator / thread model worked really well for us in practice. Generally, each movement was owned by a thread. I thought the process I used to achieve this was pretty interesting, so I decided to write it up. tl;dr - For things that can't be 'verified' as good or bad like art/music, just make the agent do more iterations, way more iterations than you think. There were three jobs: writing the symphony, turning the score into a recording, and making the video. I'll go through them in that order. Part 1: Writing the symphony Claude Opus 5.5 composed a four-movement symphony for full orchestra, about 25 minutes of music, in one month. This is how it was done. The music is written as text Claude cannot easily click notes onto a staff, but it can write text. So the whole score was written in LilyPond, a free program that turns typed text into printed sheet music. A note is a letter and a number: c4 is a C that lasts a quarter note. From the same text, LilyPond also produces a basic computer playback. Because the score is text, Claude could edit it, compare two versions, and check it with small programs, the way programmers work on code. The first theme of Movement I, as Claude typed it These eight bars for the first violins are copied from the score's source text. Point at a note in the text or on the staff to find it in the other. Click a note to hear it. Swipe the staff sideways to see all of it. tOneVlnOne = { r8 g'8 \downbow\f( es''4. ->) d''8 ( des''8 c''8 | c''4 --) b'8 \upbow( c''8 ) aes'4. -> g'8 | r8 g'8 \downbow( f''4. ->) es''8 ( d''8 c''8 | b'4 ) d''8 ( c''8 ) g'2 | r8 aes'8 \downbow\<( aes''4. ->) g''8 ( f''8 es''8 | des''8 es''8 f''8 aes''8 ) des'''2 ~\sf | des'''8 \>( c'''8 bes''8 aes''8 ) g''4. -> f''8 | es''8 ( d''8 c''8 b'8 ) c''4 \mf r4 |
} Play the recording Play only these notes "Only these notes" is a simple synthesizer in your browser playing the violin line alone. The recording is the same eight bars from the final mockup, with the whole orchestra. One Claude writes, another reviews The method is called an actor-critic loop. One Claude, the composer, writes the music. Another Claude, the critic, reviews it. The composer writes a version of a movement. A new critic, which has never seen the piece before, studies it and suggests specific fixes. The composer decides which fixes to accept. The result becomes the next version, and a new critic starts again. Each critic looked at one subject only, such as melody, harmony, the use of the instruments, or whether the parts are playable. A written log recorded every decision, so that a later critic would not undo an earlier one. Each movement went through this loop about 600 times. With four movements, that's about 2,400 rounds. Originally I only planned to do this looping process around 50 times. But once I hit 50, it seemed like the agent was still improving. So, I just kept going. This entire process was mostly autonomous. I just prompted Claude to keep going. Every round, from the written log I II III IV Each square is one round. Time runs from left to right, ten rounds per column, and each of the ten rows is one critic's subject. Pick a square to read that round's line in the log. Swipe the grid sideways to see later rounds. Loading the log… ← → A random round the log records a change the log says nothing was changed These are the real lines from the log, cut to about 200 characters. They are written in the working shorthand of the loop, with bar numbers and instrument names. Critics had to read the textbooks A critic's opinion was not enough. I supplied standard textbooks on orchestration, harmony and music notation. From then on, each critic was assigned a chapter and had to show where the score followed it and where it did not. One example: an orchestration textbook says that in loud passages two horns are needed to balance one trumpet. A critic checked every loud brass chord against that rule, found three chords where an important note was too weak, and proposed fixes. The composer accepted all three. Great symphonies as models Claude also collected scores by composers such as Beethoven, Dvořák and Tchaikovsky. Every tenth critic compared a whole movement with one movement by a master and answered one question: would a conductor believe a master wrote this? What I did I set the goal: a symphony that could pass for the work of a master, with melodies people remember. I chose the method, supplied the books and decided how long the loops ran. I left the musical choices to Claude. I was also the only one who could listen. Claude cannot hear audio, so it judged the music from the written score. I listened to computer playbacks and asked for a small number of changes. Version 1 next to the final version I thought it would be neat to compare version 1 of each movement against the final version. There are no high quality recordings of version 1, so both are played here with the same basic MIDI sounds, which is the closest thing to compare. Version 1 against the final version I II III IV The strip is the final version of the movement, one segment per bar. The brighter a bar, the more of its notes are new or changed since version 1. Click anywhere on the strip to play from that bar, and switch between the two versions while it plays. bar 1 same notes as version 1 new or changed Play Version 1 Final Go to the first theme Loading… A note counts as kept when version 1 has the same pitch at the same place in the matching bar. A pitch played by several instruments at once counts once. A program matched the bars of the two versions, so the shading is approximate. Both versions are set to the same volume. Version 1 of Movement I was a sketch for strings only, and its first theme was different. In Movements II, III and IV the main themes begin with the same notes in version 1 and in the final version, and the bright bars on the strips show where the changes are. By the count described above, about 14% of the notes in the final Movement I were already in version 1, 35% in Movement II, 61% in Movement III and 55% in Movement IV. What to keep in mind Claude wrote 99.9% of every single element of the composition. I maybe influenced a measure or two of note selection over the whole process. Part 2: Turning the score into a recording A written score doesn't make any sound, so the next job was a recording. The recordings of the symphony are not a live orchestra.

